  "summary": "Healthcare leaders are pressing CMS for more time to implement new Medicaid eligibility requirements, signaling implementation challenges ahead for states. Meanwhile, insurance denials have emerged as a significant policy concern, with lawmakers and the HHS inspector general scrutinizing AI-driven care denials in Medicare Advantage, where plans are reversing nearly all denied appeals—suggesting systemic barriers rather than appropriate gatekeeping. On the innovation front, obesity drugs continue reshaping the market with some employers planning to drop GLP-1 coverage next year as usage scales, while Novartis advanced its RNA therapy for a previously untreatable muscle disease and Abridge secured high-profile partnerships with Nvidia and Eli Lilly to strengthen its position in clinical AI. The broader healthcare AI landscape is shifting toward responsible implementation and patient-facing safeguards, with Hartford HealthCare developing safer AI interfaces and the industry grappling with liability risks as deregulation accelerates innovation. Cost pressures remain central to the conversation, with commentary noting that AI is amplifying existing structural incentives for intensity-driven spending rather than driving new inflation.",
